    {
      "filename": "Panzersperre batterie fjell.JPG",
      "title": "File:Panzersperre batterie fjell.JPG",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Panzersperre_batterie_fjell.JPG",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panzersperre_batterie_fjell.JPG",
      "width": 3456,
      "height": 2304,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/",
      "artist": "Sindre Skrede",
      "description": "A \"Panzersperre\" from WWII: eight large rocks placed on top of a concrete base, as it stands on sides of the road leading up to \"Batterie Fjell\", at Sotra island, Hordaland - Norway. Originally, the rocks were held in place by a thick wire only, which could be cut in case should enemy troops land and try to advance towards the Fjell Fortress via the road. The eight heavy rocks would, when the wire was cut, fall down on the road and block the way for tanks and cars. On the right hand side, there is a steep hill impossible to climb for tracked or wheeled vehicles, to the left there is a wetland, bog, also impossible to cross. \nThis particular roadblock is a rare construction: a german standard roadblock was named \"Walzkörpersperre\", and was made entirely of concrete. \n\nSee also a closeup: Image:Panzersperre batterie fjell closeup.JPG",
      "dateCreated": "2007-05-11",
      "categories": [
        "Anti-tank obstacles in Norway",
        "CC-BY-SA-2.5",
        "CC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated"
      ],
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "fjell-fortress-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u4ex",
      "mediumPath": "_m/u/4/e/x/fjell-fortress-wp/panzersperre-batterie-fjell-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/u/4/e/x/fjell-fortress-wp/panzersperre-batterie-fjell-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Panzersperre batterie fjell closeup.JPG",
      "title": "File:Panzersperre batterie fjell closeup.JPG",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Panzersperre_batterie_fjell_closeup.JPG",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panzersperre_batterie_fjell_closeup.JPG",
      "width": 2304,
      "height": 3456,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/",
      "artist": "Sindre Skrede",
      "description": "Closeup view of one of the stones in the \"Panzersperre\" from WWII: eight large rocks placed on top of a concrete base, placed on the sides of the road leading up to \"Batterie Fjell\", at Sotra island, Hordaland - Norway. Originally, the rocks were held in place by a thick wire only, which could be cut in case should enemy troops land and try to advance towards the Fjell Fortress via the road. The eight heavy rocks would, when the wire was cut, fall down on the road and block the way for tanks and cars. On the right hand side, there is a steep hill impossible to climb for tracked or wheeled vehicles, to the left there is a wetland, bog, also impossible to cross.\nThis particular roadblock is a rare construction: a german standard roadblock was named \"Walzkörpersperre\", and was made entirely of concrete.\nThis picture shows one of the stones and its original (today rusty) steel wire, which originally held it in place, can be seen hanging from the top of the stone and downwards somewhat to the left in the picture. \n\nSee also Image:Panzersperre batterie fjell.JPG",
      "dateCreated": "2007-05-11",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "fjell-fortress-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u4ex",
